1. What is joins  
Join is a SQL operation that combines rows from two or more tables based on a related column between them.

2. Explain types  
a) Inner join or Equi join or Simple Join: Retrieves matching rows using equal (=) operator. Example: SELECT A.X, B.X FROM TABLE_A A, TABLE_B B WHERE A.X = B.X;  
b) Non-equi Join: Retrieves non-matching rows using inequality (!= or <>). Example: SELECT A.X, B.X FROM TABLE_A A, TABLE_B B WHERE A.X != B.X;  
c) Cross product or Cartesian join: Combines all rows without any condition. Example: SELECT A.X, A.X FROM TABLE_A A, TABLE_B B;  
d) Left outer join: Retrieves all from left table and unmatched values as NULL in right. Example: SELECT A.X, B.X FROM TABLE_A A LEFT OUTER JOIN TABLE_B B ON A.X = B.X;  
e) Right outer join: Retrieves all from right table and unmatched values as NULL in left. Example: SELECT A.X, B.X FROM TABLE_A A RIGHT OUTER JOIN TABLE_B B ON A.X = B.X;  
f) Full outer join: Combines all rows from both

